A regular tetrahedron can be placed in a 3D coordinate system in several ways, but a common one is to place it such that its vertices are (1,1,1), (1,-1,-1), (-1,1,-1), and (-1,-1,1). This configuration helps simplify the calculations.
</result>

<python>
import numpy as np

# Define the coordinates of the vertices of the tetrahedron
A = np.array([1, 1, 1])
B = np.array([1, -1, -1])
C = np.array([-1, 1, -1])
D = np.array([-1, -1, 1])

# Find the midpoints M and N
M = (A + B) / 2
N = (C + D) / 2

# Calculate the vectors MN and AD
MN = N - M
AD = D - A

# Calculate the cosine of the angle between MN and AD
cos_theta = np.dot(MN, AD) / (np.linalg.norm(MN) * np.linalg.norm(AD))

# Calculate the angle in degrees
theta = np.degrees(np.arccos(cos_theta))
theta
</python>
