<think>
The first derivative of \( f(x) = e^x - ax - 1 \) is \( f'(x) = e^x - a \).

(1) To find the intervals where \( f(x) \) is increasing, we need to find where \( f'(x) > 0 \):
\[ e^x - a > 0 \Rightarrow e^x > a \Rightarrow x > \ln(a) \]
So, \( f(x) \) is increasing on \( (\ln(a), \infty) \).

(2) For \( f(x) \) to be monotonically increasing on \( \mathbb{R} \), \( f'(x) \) must be non-negative everywhere:
\[ e^x - a \geq 0 \Rightarrow a \leq e^x \]
This inequality must hold for all \( x \in \mathbb{R} \). Since \( e^x \) can take any positive value, the maximum value of \( a \) is zero, so \( a \leq 0 \).

(3) We need to find if there's a value of \( a \) such that \( f(x) \) is decreasing on \( (-\infty, 0] \) and increasing on \( [0, \infty) \). This means \( f'(x) \leq 0 \) for \( x \leq 0 \) and \( f'(x) \geq 0 \) for \( x \geq 0 \):
\[ e^x - a \leq 0 \text{ for } x \leq 0 \Rightarrow a \geq e^x \]
\[ e^x - a \geq 0 \text{ for } x \geq 0 \Rightarrow a \leq e^x \]
Evaluating at \( x = 0 \):
\[ a \geq e^0 = 1 \]
\[ a \leq e^0 = 1 \]
So, the only value of \( a \) that satisfies both conditions is \( a = 1 \).
</think>

<answer>
(1) The intervals where \( f(x) \) is monotonically increasing are \( (\ln(a), \infty) \).
(2) The range of possible values for \( a \) if \( f(x) \) is monotonically increasing on \( \mathbb{R} \) is \( a \leq 0 \).
(3) The value of \( a \) such that \( f(x) \) is monotonically decreasing on \( (-\infty, 0] \) and monotonically increasing on \( [0, +\infty) \) is \( a = 1 \).

