The method of Lagrange multipliers is a strategy for finding the local maxima and minima of a function subject to equality constraints. It involves introducing a new variable (the Lagrange multiplier) and solving a system of equations.

For a function \( f(x, y) \) subject to a constraint \( g(x, y) = c \), we introduce a new function \( \mathcal{L}(x, y, \lambda) = f(x, y) - \lambda (g(x, y) - c) \), and find the critical points by solving the system of equations given by \(\nabla \mathcal{L} = 0\).

