<answer>
From the derivative \(f'(x) = e^x - a\):

(1) For \(f(x)\) to be monotonically increasing, solve \(f'(x) \geq 0\):
\[ e^x - a \geq 0 \implies e^x \geq a \implies x \geq \ln(a), \text{ for } a > 0. \]
For \(a \leq 0\), \(f'(x) > 0 \text{ for all } x\).

(2) For \(f(x)\) to be monotonically increasing on \(\mathbb{R}\), \(a \leq 0\).

(3) For \(f(x)\) to be monotonically decreasing on \((- \infty, 0]\) and monotonically increasing on \([0, + \infty)\), set \(f'(x)\):

On \((- \infty, 0]\), \(f'(x) = e^x - a \leq 0 \implies e^x \leq a \implies a \geq e^0 = 1\).
On \([0, +\infty)\), \(f'(x) = e^x - a \geq 0 \implies e^x \geq a\).

The value \(a = 1\) satisfies both conditions and makes \(f'(x)\) change sign at \(x = 0\).

Therefore, the value of \(a\) is \(a = 1\).

\boxed{1}
</answer>, ground_truth: a = 1
